From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:04:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/27] feat(mcp): agents start comment threads with
 add_comment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

reply_to_comment answers a thread, but an agent could not open one: a
review agent that finds a problem in a frame had no way to pin it where
people look. add_comment is the MCP side of POST /api/frames/:id/comments
and POST /api/canvases/:id/comments.

- frame_id + element_id (the data-doop-id from get_frame) or selector (an
  "#id > …" or "body > …" path, stamped first) pins it to an element;
  frame_id + x, y to a point in the frame; frame_id alone to the frame;
  page_id + x, y to the canvas.
- An element that is not in the saved HTML is an error. The browser falls
  back to a frame comment when its pin drifted; an agent named the element
  on purpose and should hear that it is wrong.
- The snippet is the element's saved markup, cut like the runtime's probe
  (400 chars), so the thread, get_comments and webhooks show what it was.
- Same rules as reply_to_comment: comment access; an @mention of a resident
  role needs edit and spends one resident task, refunded if the post fails;
  written as the agent (actor kind agent), billed to the connecting user.

addElementComment and addCanvasComment take the actor kind, so the activity
log and the comment.created event say agent rather than user.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
